---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
// Matty's curated Canonical_DB, a bounded "first prototype corpus" (Guide §8), run through
// the real 0.2 pipeline and stored to kb-handoff/ — kept distinct from the article corpus
// (data/kb/) per his Database_Spec ("do not collapse into one universal table").
import after from "../../kb-handoff/index.json";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const withBase = (p) => base + String(p).replace(/^\//, "");

const total = after.total;
const reviewQueue = after.review_queue ?? after.reviewQueue;
const types = Object.entries(after.by_type)
  .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
  .map(([type, n]) => `${n} ${type.replace(/-/g, " ")}${n > 1 ? "s" : ""}`);
---

<Page
  title="Handoff slice · Regen Web3 Toolkit"
  description="Matty's curated master-doc database, a bounded prototype corpus, run through the toolkit's own machine — the honest before/after."
>
  <section class="in-hero">
    <div class="wrap in-hero__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal" data-d="1">Handoff · convergence</p>
      <h1 class="in-hero__title reveal" data-d="2">
        Matt's database,<br />run through the machine.
      </h1>
      <p class="in-hero__lede reveal" data-d="3">
        Matt's July master-doc handoff includes a large curated database — and a spec that,
        remarkably, describes the same capture → review → publish pipeline this toolkit already runs.
        To test that, we fed a <strong>bounded prototype corpus</strong> of his own Canonical_DB
        (127 curated rows across 5 object families) through the machine → <strong>{total} typed
        objects</strong>, each preserving his text and source lineage, all <code>raw</code> in the
        review queue. Nothing promoted — a human reviews before anything is published.
      </p>
      <div class="in-hero__meta reveal" data-d="4">
        <span class="chip chip--clay">127 curated rows → {total} objects</span>
        <span class="in-hero__metasep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>from Canonical_DB</span>
        <span class="in-hero__metasep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>all raw</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="in-reg">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="in-sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">What his data came out as</p>
        <h2 class="in-h2">Six object families, typed through his own crosswalk.</h2>
        <p class="in-sec-sub">
          His Source-System Cards, New Objects, Options, Claims &amp; Cautions, and Implementation
          Memory sheets, typed via his own normalization crosswalk and run through the accept gate.
          Relationships were deliberately left parked — the framework doesn't yet carry a first-class
          per-edge record (an honest gap, not papered over).
        </p>
      </header>
      <ul class="in-stat-grid">
        <li class="in-stat">
          <span class="in-stat__n">{total}</span>
          <span class="in-stat__label">typed objects</span>
          <span class="in-stat__desc">From 127 curated Canonical_DB rows, read from the resulting index.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="in-stat">
          <span class="in-stat__n">{reviewQueue}</span>
          <span class="in-stat__label">in the review queue</span>
          <span class="in-stat__desc">All <code>raw</code>; nothing promoted or published.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ul class="in-run__types">
        {types.map((t) => <li class="in-run__type">{t}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="in-held">
    <div class="wrap in-held__grid">
      <div class="in-held__card">
        <p class="eyebrow">The convergence moment</p>
        <h3 class="in-held__h">His hand-flagged duplicates, caught automatically.</h3>
        <p>
          Matt's database hand-flags <strong>4 duplicate source-system pairs</strong>. Ingesting all
          67 of his cards, the machine's overwrite guard caught the <strong>same 4</strong> on its own
          — preserving both sides of each rather than letting one silently overwrite the other. His
          curated dedup and the automatic guard reached the same answer, on his own data.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="in-held__card">
        <p class="eyebrow">The public/private caveat, handled</p>
        <h3 class="in-held__h">Sensitive rows flagged at the gate.</h3>
        <p>
          Matt noted the handoff wasn't reeled in for public vs private. Unprompted, the machine raised
          <strong>public-use boundaries</strong> on exactly the sensitive rows — Indigenous territory,
          gender-violence case data, a children's publication. Nothing sensitive reaches a public view
          without review.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="in-cross">
    <div class="wrap in-cross__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">See the rest</p>
      <h2 class="in-h2">The machine, the toolkit's own run, and how it maps.</h2>
      <p class="in-cross__lede">
        This is one prototype run on Matt's database. For the toolkit's own 119 articles through the
        same machine, see the self-ingestion page; for how the pipeline works, the framework page.
      </p>
      <div class="in-cross__cta">
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={withBase("/self-ingestion/")}>The toolkit's own run <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={withBase("/framework/")}>How the pipeline works <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
      </div>
      <p class="in-cross__honest">
        Honest status: a bounded prototype slice of ~2,689 curated rows, all <strong>raw</strong> and
        review-gated — the full migration and the human review pass come next.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
